In re Microsoft Corp. (“Microsoft I”)
_________________________

No. 1:13–MJ–02814, 2014 BL 242947 (S.D.N.Y. August 29, 2014)

LORETTA A. PRESKA, DISTRICT JUDGE.

MEMORANDUM AND ORDER

Before the Court is the Government’s motion to lift the stay in execution of the Court’s July 31, 2014 order (the “July 31 Order”) affirming the April 25, 2014 decision of Magistrate Judge James C. Francis IV. For the reasons set forth below, that motion is GRANTED.

I. BACKGROUND

On December 4, 2013, Judge Francis issued a warrant (the “Warrant”) under the Stored Communications Act directing Microsoft Corporation (“Microsoft”) to disclose records within its control pertaining to a specific email account. [This warrant had been requested by the Government as part of a criminal investigation into narcotics trafficking. The request was pursuant to the Stored Communication Act, 18 U.S.C. §§ 2701–2712, which authorizes the issuance of subpoenas and search warrants for electronically stored communications held by internet service providers and others.—ed.] On December 18, 2013, Microsoft moved to vacate the Warrant to the extent that the Warrant authorized a search and seizure of content stored in a datacenter in Dublin, Ireland. On April 25, 2014, Judge Francis denied that motion. * * *

On June 6, 2014, Microsoft brought the matter before this Court by objecting to Judge Francis’s ruling. On July 31, 2014, the Court heard oral argument on those objections and affirmed Judge Francis’s ruling by issuing the July 31 Order on the record. * * * On August 11, 2014, the Court entered a written order confirming the July 31 Order. That same day, Microsoft filed a notice of appeal.

On August 12, 2014, the Government brought the instant motion. There, the Government asserts that jurisdiction over the instant case still lies in this Court because the July 31 Order “is not a final, appealable order and appellate review is unavailable at this stage of the litigation.” The Government thus proposes that the Court direct Microsoft to comply with the July 31 Order. If Microsoft refuses to comply, the Court could find Microsoft in contempt, which would be a final order subject to appellate review. Microsoft opposes, arguing
702
that the July 31 Order “is appealable” and, in any event, that the Government’s jurisdictional concerns are “better addressed to the [Court of Appeals], which has jurisdiction over this case” due to, inter alia, Microsoft’s having filed a notice of appeal. Both parties share the common goal of permitting the Court of Appeals to hear this case as soon as possible. Their disagreement concerns the correct path to that goal. In order words, the parties agree on the destination but the route to get there is the subject of hot dispute.

II. DISCUSSION

* * *

The Government argues that the July 31 Order is not a final order subject to appeal at this stage in the litigation.

“Finality as a condition of review is an historic characteristic of federal appellate procedure.” Thus, a party is ordinarily “entitled to a single appeal, to be deferred until final judgment has been entered, in which claims of district court error at any stage of the litigation may be ventilated.” Following from this, 28 U.S.C. § 1291 grants the courts of appeals “jurisdiction of appeals from all final decisions of the district courts of the United States ….”

In this circuit, an order denying a motion to quash a * * * subpoena is not a final decision and is, therefore, not immediately appealable under 28 U.S.C. § 1291. “This general rule applies whether the subpoena is issued in connection with civil and criminal actions, or grand jury proceedings, and whether the person (or entity) seeking to prevent enforcement of the subpoena is a party to the litigation or a non-party witness.” In such instances, “[t]o obtain appellate review, the subpoenaed person ordinarily must defy the district court’s enforcement order, be held in contempt, and then appeal the contempt order, which is regarded as final under § 1291.”

* * *

III. Conclusion

For the foregoing reasons, the Government’s motion to lift the stay in execution of the Court’s July 31, 2014 order is GRANTED.

The parties shall confer and inform the Court by joint letter no later than September 5, 2014 as to how they propose to proceed.

703
So Ordered.



•Denial of a motion to quash a subpoena is not a final judgment and thus not immediately appealable

•A party can obtain immediate appeal of such an order by refusing to comply and being held in contempt, as the contempt order is a final decision

